{"id":"task-api-completed-filter","task":"Implement GET /tasks?completed=true query filter that returns only completed tasks, respecting existing repository architecture.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"memories":[{"key":"task-repo-constraint","type":"constraint","content":"Task API route handlers must never directly access the SQLite database. All persistence operations go through the TaskRepository interface.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"task-repo-decision","type":"decision","content":"Database access must go through repository interfaces.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"task-sqlite","type":"decision","content":"task-api uses SQLite.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"task-express","type":"decision","content":"task-api uses Express with TypeScript strict mode.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"global-plural-rule","type":"rule","content":"REST API resources should use plural nouns in endpoint paths, such as /tasks and /notes.","scope":{"kind":"global","id":null},"confidence":0.5}],"labels":{"task-repo-constraint":2,"task-repo-decision":2,"task-sqlite":1,"task-express":1,"global-plural-rule":1},"redundantWith":{"task-repo-decision":"task-repo-constraint"},"notes":"The case Phase 1 got RIGHT: the retrieved repository constraint caused filtering to be placed in TaskRepository/SqliteTaskRepository rather than in the route handler. Included so a strategy cannot win by simply always preferring constraints -- the stack decisions are genuinely secondary-relevant here, and the global naming rule is relevant because the endpoint is being named."}
{"id":"task-api-convention-check","task":"What REST endpoint naming convention should we follow?","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"memories":[{"key":"global-plural-rule","type":"rule","content":"REST API resources should use plural nouns in endpoint paths, such as /tasks and /notes.","scope":{"kind":"global","id":null},"confidence":0.5},{"key":"task-repo-constraint","type":"constraint","content":"Task API route handlers must never directly access the SQLite database. All persistence operations go through the TaskRepository interface.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"task-sqlite","type":"decision","content":"task-api uses SQLite.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0}],"labels":{"global-plural-rule":2,"task-repo-constraint":0,"task-sqlite":0},"redundantWith":{},"notes":"The counterexample that forbids 'project always beats global' (decision E9). task-api has no project-level naming rule, so the GLOBAL memory is the single correct answer and must rank first. Any scope-specificity rule strong enough to demote global knowledge fails this example."}
{"id":"isolation-notes-vs-task","task":"How should database access be structured in this project?","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"notes-api"},"memories":[{"key":"notes-repo-constraint","type":"constraint","content":"notes-api: database access must go through the repository layer (NotesRepository interface); route handlers must never touch the database directly","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"notes-api"},"confidence":1.0},{"key":"task-repo-constraint","type":"constraint","content":"Task API route handlers must never directly access the SQLite database. All persistence operations go through the TaskRepository interface.","scope":{"kind":"project","id":"task-api"},"confidence":1.0}],"labels":{"notes-repo-constraint":2,"task-repo-constraint":0},"redundantWith":{},"notes":"Scope-isolation counterexample. The task-api constraint is near-identical in content and would score highly on pure relevance, so a retrieval layer that leaks across scopes shows up here as a precision loss rather than passing silently. A correct implementation never returns it at all."}
